/-!
# Cutting a Cohen–Macaulay equidimensional affine algebra down by the right dimension

Let `k` be a field, let `S` be a Cohen–Macaulay finite type `k`-algebra which is equidimensional
of dimension `d`, and let `f = f₁, …, f_i` be elements of `S` generating a proper ideal with
`dim V(f₁, …, f_i) ≤ d - i`. This file proves Stacks 00R9: the inequality is an equality and
`f₁, …, f_i` is a regular sequence in `S_𝔮` for every prime `𝔮 ⊇ (f₁, …, f_i)`.

Both halves are Krull's height theorem read at a closed point. Equidimensionality makes every
maximal ideal `𝔪` of `S` have height `dim S` (`height_eq_ringKrullDim_of_isMaximal`), so Krull's
height theorem at a maximal ideal of `V(f₁, …, f_i)` gives `d ≤ dim V(f₁, …, f_i) + i`, which is
the missing inequality. At such an `𝔪` the same two facts say that `f₁, …, f_i` cuts the
dimension of the Cohen–Macaulay local ring `S_𝔪` down by exactly `i`, so `f₁, …, f_i` is a
regular sequence in `S_𝔪` by Lemma 02JN, the case `M = R` of Proposition 00N6; and a regular
sequence in `S_𝔪` whose members lie in a prime `𝔮 ≤ 𝔪` stays regular in `S_𝔮`, a localisation
of `S_𝔪`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.CohenMacaulay.EquidimensionalCut.solution`: the statement of Stacks 00R9.

## Implementation notes

The source deduces `dim(S_𝔪) = d` at every maximal ideal from Lemma 00OV, the local constancy of
the local dimension; that is more than is needed, and `height_eq_ringKrullDim_of_isMaximal`
derives it from Lemma 00OT instead, whose two descriptions of the local dimension — as a supremum
over the components through the point and as an infimum of heights of maximal ideals over it —
give it in two steps.

The source also restricts the regular-sequence conclusion to maximal ideals and localises once
more to reach an arbitrary prime. That localisation is `isRegular_of_isWeaklyRegular_localization`
here, carried out at the statement's arbitrary model `Sq` of `S_𝔮`, so no transport along
`IsLocalization.algEquiv` occurs.

Two general facts used along the way have no counterpart in Mathlib and are proved here:
`height_le_ringKrullDim_quotient_ofList_add_length`, Krull's height theorem for the ideal
generated by a list, and `ringKrullDim_quotient_map_le`, that `V(I)` does not grow in dimension
when `R` is replaced by a localisation of `R`.

## References

* [StacksProject 00R9](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00R9)
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace Stacks.CohenMacaulay.EquidimensionalCut

open IsLocalRing RingTheory.Sequence TopologicalSpace

section Dimension

/-- **Krull's height theorem for the ideal generated by a list.** A prime containing
`(f₁, …, f_i)` has height at most `dim V(f₁, …, f_i) + i`. -/
public theorem height_le_ringKrullDim_quotient_ofList_add_length {R : Type*} [CommRing R]
    [IsNoetherianRing R] {p : Ideal R} [p.IsPrime] {f : List R} (hf : Ideal.ofList f ≤ p) :
    (p.height : WithBot ℕ∞) ≤ ringKrullDim (R ⧸ Ideal.ofList f) + f.length := by
  classical
  refine (height_le_ringKrullDim_quotient_add_spanFinrank hf).trans (add_le_add le_rfl ?_)
  have hle : (Ideal.ofList f).spanFinrank ≤ f.length := by
    refine (Submodule.spanFinrank_span_le_ncard_of_finite f.finite_toSet).trans ?_
    rw [show {x | x ∈ f} = (f.toFinset : Set R) by simp, Set.ncard_coe_finset]
    exact List.toFinset_card_le f
  exact_mod_cast hle

/-- **The Krull dimension of a closed subset does not grow under localisation.** If `A` is a
localisation of `R` at a submonoid `T` and `I` is an ideal of `R`, then `V(IA) ⊆ Spec A` has
dimension at most that of `V(I) ⊆ Spec R`, because `Spec A → Spec R` is an order embedding
carrying `V(IA)` into `V(I)`. -/
@[ega "0 16.1.7" "special case"]
private theorem ringKrullDim_quotient_map_le {R A : Type*} [CommRing R] [CommRing A] [Algebra R A]
    (T : Submonoid R) [IsLocalization T A] (I : Ideal R) :
    ringKrullDim (A ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R A)) ≤ ringKrullDim (R ⧸ I) := by
  rw [ringKrullDim_quotient, ringKrullDim_quotient]
  refine Order.krullDim_le_of_strictMono
    (fun p => ⟨PrimeSpectrum.comap (algebraMap R A) p.1, ?_⟩) (fun {p p'} h => ?_)
  · exact Ideal.map_le_iff_le_comap.mp p.2
  · refine lt_of_le_of_ne (Ideal.comap_mono h.le) fun he => ?_
    exact absurd (Subtype.ext (PrimeSpectrum.localization_comap_injective A T
      (Subtype.ext_iff.mp he))) (ne_of_lt h)

/-- **On an equidimensional `Spec S` the local dimension is constant, equal to `dim S`.** By
Lemma 00OT the local dimension at a point is the supremum of the dimensions of the irreducible
components through it; equidimensionality collapses that supremum to the dimension of any one
component, so the local dimension is independent of the point, and
`topologicalKrullDim_eq_iSup_localKrullDim` identifies its common value with `dim Spec S`. -/
public theorem localKrullDim_eq_ringKrullDim (k : Type*) {S : Type*} [Field k] [CommRing S]
    [Algebra k S] [Algebra.FiniteType k S] (hequi : Equidimensional (PrimeSpectrum S))
    (x : PrimeSpectrum S) : localKrullDim (PrimeSpectrum S) x = ringKrullDim S := by
  have hcomp : ∀ y : PrimeSpectrum S,
      localKrullDim (PrimeSpectrum S) y = topologicalKrullDim (irreducibleComponent y) := by
    intro y
    rw [(PrimeSpectrum.localKrullDim_eq_iSup_irreducibleComponents_and_eq_iInf_height k y).1]
    refine le_antisymm (iSup₂_le fun Z hZ => le_of_eq (hequi Z hZ.1 _
        (irreducibleComponent_mem_irreducibleComponents y)))
      (le_iSup₂_of_le (irreducibleComponent y)
        ⟨irreducibleComponent_mem_irreducibleComponents y, mem_irreducibleComponent⟩ le_rfl)
  rw [← PrimeSpectrum.topologicalKrullDim_eq_ringKrullDim,
    topologicalKrullDim_eq_iSup_localKrullDim]
  refine le_antisymm (le_iSup _ x) (iSup_le fun y => ?_)
  rw [hcomp y, hcomp x]
  exact le_of_eq (hequi _ (irreducibleComponent_mem_irreducibleComponents y) _
    (irreducibleComponent_mem_irreducibleComponents x))

/-- **At a closed point the local dimension of `Spec S` is the height of the point.** By
Lemma 00OT the local dimension at a point is the infimum of the heights of the maximal ideals
containing it, and a maximal ideal is contained in no other. -/
private theorem localKrullDim_eq_height_of_isMaximal (k : Type*) {S : Type*} [Field k]
    [CommRing S] [Algebra k S] [Algebra.FiniteType k S] (𝔪 : Ideal S) (h𝔪 : 𝔪.IsMaximal) :
    localKrullDim (PrimeSpectrum S) ⟨𝔪, h𝔪.isPrime⟩ = (𝔪.height : WithBot ℕ∞) := by
  rw [(PrimeSpectrum.localKrullDim_eq_iSup_irreducibleComponents_and_eq_iInf_height k
    (⟨𝔪, h𝔪.isPrime⟩ : PrimeSpectrum S)).2]
  refine le_antisymm (iInf₂_le 𝔪 ⟨h𝔪, le_rfl⟩) (le_iInf₂ fun 𝔫 h => le_of_eq ?_)
  rw [h𝔪.eq_of_le h.1.ne_top h.2]

/-- **Every closed point of an equidimensional `Spec S` has height `dim S`.** This is what the
source takes from Lemma 00OV: `dim(S_𝔪) = d` for every maximal ideal `𝔪` of `S`. -/
private theorem height_eq_ringKrullDim_of_isMaximal (k : Type*) {S : Type*} [Field k] [CommRing S]
    [Algebra k S] [Algebra.FiniteType k S] (hequi : Equidimensional (PrimeSpectrum S))
    (𝔪 : Ideal S) [h𝔪 : 𝔪.IsMaximal] : (𝔪.height : WithBot ℕ∞) = ringKrullDim S :=
  (localKrullDim_eq_height_of_isMaximal k 𝔪 h𝔪).symm.trans
    (localKrullDim_eq_ringKrullDim k hequi _)

end Dimension

section Regular

/-- The image of a list of elements of a prime `q` of `S` lies in the maximal ideal of any model
of `S_q`. -/
private theorem forall_mem_maximalIdeal_map_algebraMap {S : Type*} [CommRing S] (q : Ideal S)
    [q.IsPrime] (Sq : Type*) [CommRing Sq] [Algebra S Sq] [IsLocalization.AtPrime Sq q]
    [IsLocalRing Sq] {f : List S} (hf : Ideal.ofList f ≤ q) :
    ∀ r ∈ f.map (algebraMap S Sq), r ∈ maximalIdeal Sq := by
  intro r hr
  obtain ⟨x, hx, rfl⟩ := List.mem_map.mp hr
  exact (IsLocalization.AtPrime.to_map_mem_maximal_iff Sq q x).mpr (hf (Ideal.subset_span hx))

/-- **Lemma 02JN at a closed point of `V(f₁, …, f_i)`.** At a maximal ideal `𝔪 ⊇ (f₁, …, f_i)`
the local ring `S_𝔪` is Cohen–Macaulay of dimension `dim S`
(`height_eq_ringKrullDim_of_isMaximal`), and `(f₁, …, f_i)` cuts that dimension down by exactly
`i`: by at least `i` because localising only shrinks `V(f₁, …, f_i)`, and by at most `i` by
Krull's height theorem. So `f₁, …, f_i` is a regular sequence in `S_𝔪` by Lemma 02JN. -/
private theorem isRegular_localizationAtPrime (k : Type*) {S : Type*} [Field k] [CommRing S]
    [Algebra k S] [Algebra.FiniteType k S] [IsNoetherianRing S]
    (hS : ∀ (p : Ideal S) [p.IsPrime],
      Module.IsMaximalCohenMacaulay (Localization.AtPrime p) (Localization.AtPrime p))
    (hequi : Equidimensional (PrimeSpectrum S)) {f : List S}
    (hdim : ringKrullDim (S ⧸ Ideal.ofList f) + f.length ≤ ringKrullDim S)
    (𝔪 : Ideal S) [𝔪.IsMaximal] (hfle : Ideal.ofList f ≤ 𝔪) :
    IsRegular (Localization.AtPrime 𝔪) (f.map (algebraMap S (Localization.AtPrime 𝔪))) := by
  have hnoeth : IsNoetherianRing (Localization.AtPrime 𝔪) :=
    IsLocalization.isNoetherianRing 𝔪.primeCompl (Localization.AtPrime 𝔪) ‹_›
  have hmem := forall_mem_maximalIdeal_map_algebraMap 𝔪 (Localization.AtPrime 𝔪) hfle
  refine ((hS 𝔪).isRegular_iff_ringKrullDim_add_length_eq_ringKrullDim _ hmem).1.mpr
    (le_antisymm ?_ ?_)
  · rw [IsLocalization.AtPrime.ringKrullDim_eq_height 𝔪 (Localization.AtPrime 𝔪),
      height_eq_ringKrullDim_of_isMaximal k hequi 𝔪, ← Ideal.map_ofList, List.length_map]
    exact le_trans (add_le_add (ringKrullDim_quotient_map_le 𝔪.primeCompl (Ideal.ofList f)) le_rfl)
      hdim
  · rw [← maximalIdeal_height_eq_ringKrullDim]
    exact height_le_ringKrullDim_quotient_ofList_add_length (Submodule.span_le.mpr hmem)

/-- **Regularity passes from `S_𝔪` down to `S_𝔮` for a prime `𝔮 ≤ 𝔪`.** Any model `Sq` of `S_𝔮`
is a localisation of `S_𝔪`, so a weakly regular sequence in `S_𝔪` stays weakly regular in `Sq`,
and there it is regular because its members lie in the maximal ideal. -/
private theorem isRegular_of_isWeaklyRegular_localization {S : Type*} [CommRing S] {q 𝔪 : Ideal S}
    [q.IsPrime] [𝔪.IsPrime] (hle : q ≤ 𝔪) {f : List S} (hq : Ideal.ofList f ≤ q)
    (hreg : IsWeaklyRegular (Localization.AtPrime 𝔪)
      (f.map (algebraMap S (Localization.AtPrime 𝔪))))
    (Sq : Type*) [CommRing Sq] [Algebra S Sq] [IsLocalization.AtPrime Sq q] :
    IsRegular Sq (f.map (algebraMap S Sq)) := by
  have : IsLocalRing Sq := IsLocalization.AtPrime.isLocalRing Sq q
  have hcompl : 𝔪.primeCompl ≤ q.primeCompl := fun x hx hx' => hx (hle hx')
  let algSq : Algebra (Localization.AtPrime 𝔪) Sq :=
    IsLocalization.localizationAlgebraOfSubmonoidLe _ Sq 𝔪.primeCompl q.primeCompl hcompl
  have : IsScalarTower S (Localization.AtPrime 𝔪) Sq :=
    IsLocalization.localization_isScalarTower_of_submonoid_le _ Sq 𝔪.primeCompl q.primeCompl hcompl
  have : IsLocalization (q.primeCompl.map (algebraMap S (Localization.AtPrime 𝔪))) Sq :=
    IsLocalization.isLocalization_of_submonoid_le _ Sq 𝔪.primeCompl q.primeCompl hcompl
  have hw := hreg.of_isLocalization Sq (q.primeCompl.map (algebraMap S (Localization.AtPrime 𝔪)))
  rw [List.map_map] at hw
  simp only [Function.comp_def, ← IsScalarTower.algebraMap_apply] at hw
  have : Nontrivial Sq := IsLocalization.AtPrime.nontrivial Sq q
  exact (IsLocalRing.isRegular_iff_isWeaklyRegular_of_subset_maximalIdeal
    (forall_mem_maximalIdeal_map_algebraMap q Sq hq)).mpr hw

end Regular

/-- **Stacks 00R9.** -/
public theorem solution
    {k S : Type*} [Field k] [CommRing S] [Algebra k S] [Algebra.FiniteType k S]
    (hS : ∀ (p : Ideal S) [p.IsPrime],
      Module.IsMaximalCohenMacaulay (Localization.AtPrime p) (Localization.AtPrime p))
    (hequi : TopologicalSpace.Equidimensional (PrimeSpectrum S)) (d : ℕ)
    (hd : ringKrullDim S = d) (f : List S) (hf : Ideal.ofList f ≠ ⊤)
    (hdim : ringKrullDim (S ⧸ Ideal.ofList f) + f.length ≤ (d : WithBot ℕ∞)) :
    ringKrullDim (S ⧸ Ideal.ofList f) + f.length = (d : WithBot ℕ∞) ∧
      ∀ (q : Ideal S) [q.IsPrime], Ideal.ofList f ≤ q →
        ∀ (Sq : Type*) [CommRing Sq] [Algebra S Sq] [IsLocalization.AtPrime Sq q],
          RingTheory.Sequence.IsRegular Sq (f.map (algebraMap S Sq)) := by
  have : IsNoetherianRing S := Algebra.FiniteType.isNoetherianRing k S
  have hdim' : ringKrullDim (S ⧸ Ideal.ofList f) + f.length ≤ ringKrullDim S := by
    rw [hd]; exact hdim
  refine ⟨le_antisymm hdim ?_, ?_⟩
  · -- `d ≤ dim V(f₁, …, f_i) + i`, Krull's height theorem at a maximal ideal of `V(f₁, …, f_i)`.
    obtain ⟨𝔪, h𝔪, hle⟩ := Ideal.exists_le_maximal _ hf
    have : 𝔪.IsMaximal := h𝔪
    rw [← hd, ← height_eq_ringKrullDim_of_isMaximal k hequi 𝔪]
    exact height_le_ringKrullDim_quotient_ofList_add_length hle
  · -- Pass to a closed point `𝔪 ⊇ 𝔮` of `V(f₁, …, f_i)`, then localise once more.
    intro q _ hq Sq _ _ _
    obtain ⟨𝔪, h𝔪, hle⟩ := Ideal.exists_le_maximal q ‹q.IsPrime›.ne_top
    have : 𝔪.IsMaximal := h𝔪
    exact isRegular_of_isWeaklyRegular_localization hle hq
      (isRegular_localizationAtPrime k hS hequi hdim' 𝔪 (hq.trans hle)).toIsWeaklyRegular Sq

end Stacks.CohenMacaulay.EquidimensionalCut

end

/-!
# Cutting a Cohen–Macaulay equidimensional affine algebra down by the right dimension

Let `k` be a field and let `S` be a finite type `k`-algebra which is Cohen–Macaulay and
equidimensional of dimension `d`. Let `f₁, …, f_i ∈ S`. The closed subset
`V(f₁, …, f_i) ⊆ Spec S` always has dimension at least `d - i`, since each of the `i` equations
can cut the dimension down by at most one. This file states the converse half of that
dichotomy: if `V(f₁, …, f_i)` has dimension *at most* `d - i`, then its dimension is exactly
`d - i`, and the reason is local and much stronger — `f₁, …, f_i` is a regular sequence in the
local ring `S_𝔮` at every point `𝔮` of `V(f₁, …, f_i)`.

This is the global form of the characterisation of regular sequences in a Cohen–Macaulay local
ring by the dimension they cut out (Lemma 02JN, itself the case `M = R` of Proposition 00N6).
The passage from the local statement to the global one is exactly the input that `k` is a field
and `S` of finite type over it: `Spec S` is then a Jacobson space of finite dimension, and
Cohen–Macaulayness together with equidimensionality force `dim(S_𝔪) = d` at *every* maximal
ideal `𝔪` (Lemma 00OV), which is what lets Proposition 00N6 be applied at each closed point of
`V(f₁, …, f_i)`.

## Main results

* `Module.IsMaximalCohenMacaulay.ringKrullDim_quotient_add_length_eq_and_isRegular`: the
  dimension inequality `dim V(f₁, …, f_i) + i ≤ d` is an equality, and `f₁, …, f_i` is a regular
  sequence in every localization of `S` at a prime containing `(f₁, …, f_i)`.

## Implementation notes

Cohen–Macaulayness of a Noetherian local ring is `Module.IsMaximalCohenMacaulay A A` (tag 00NF),
that is `depth_{𝔪_A}(A) = dim A`, which is Definition 00N8 verbatim; that of the non-local
Noetherian ring `S` (Definition 00NC) has no name of its own in this project and none in Mathlib,
so it is spelled out as a `∀` over the primes of `S` at the concrete `Localization.AtPrime`,
exactly as in the statement of tag 00ND. Assuming it only for `Localization.AtPrime` is the
weakest form of "`S` is Cohen–Macaulay": a reader holding the property at some other model of
`S_𝔭` instantiates it there. Dually the regular-sequence conclusion is stated for an *arbitrary*
`Sq` with `IsLocalization.AtPrime Sq q`, the strongest form, so no user has to transport along
`IsLocalization.algEquiv`. `[IsNoetherianRing S]` is not assumed: it follows from
`Algebra.FiniteType k S` over a field.

"Equidimensional of dimension `d`" is `TopologicalSpace.Equidimensional (PrimeSpectrum S)`, the
project's Definition 0058 which Lemma 00OV cites, together with `ringKrullDim S = d`. The two
together say that every irreducible component of `Spec S` has dimension `d`: the components have
a common dimension by the first, and their supremum is `dim Spec S = dim S = d`
(`PrimeSpectrum.topologicalKrullDim_eq_ringKrullDim`) by the second. Naming `d` through
`ringKrullDim S` rather than through the components also pins `S` nontrivial, since
`ringKrullDim` of the zero ring is `⊥`.

`dim V(f₁, …, f_i)` is `ringKrullDim (S ⧸ Ideal.ofList f)`, which is the Krull dimension of the
closed subset `V(f₁, …, f_i)` of `Spec S` on the nose: `ringKrullDim_quotient` says
`ringKrullDim (S ⧸ I) = Order.krullDim (PrimeSpectrum.zeroLocus I)`. The source's `f₁, …, f_i` is
a `List S`, so `i` is `f.length` and `(f₁, …, f_i)` is `Ideal.ofList f`; this is the shape
Mathlib's regular sequence API (`RingTheory.Sequence.IsRegular`) is phrased in, and it covers
`i = 0` as the empty list.

The source writes the dimension hypothesis as `dim V(f₁, …, f_i) ≤ d - i` and the conclusion as
the corresponding equality. Both are read here as `dim V(f₁, …, f_i) + i ≤ d` and
`dim V(f₁, …, f_i) + i = d`, with the subtraction moved to the other side. This is not cosmetic:
`d - i` truncated at `0` would make the statement false, as `S = k`, `d = 0`, `f = [0]` shows —
there `dim V(f) = 0 ≤ 0 = d - i` in truncated arithmetic while `0` is not a regular sequence in
`k`. The source means the difference of integers, which is what `+ i ≤ d` expresses in
`WithBot ℕ∞`.

The hypothesis `Ideal.ofList f ≠ ⊤`, that is `V(f₁, …, f_i) ≠ ∅`
(`PrimeSpectrum.zeroLocus_empty_iff_eq_top`), is needed for the dimension equality and is not in
the source's list: for `f = [1]` one has `dim V(f) = ⊥` and the hypothesis `⊥ + i ≤ d` holds
while the asserted equality `⊥ = d` does not. It is the only degenerate case, the
regular-sequence half being vacuous there, and it is a defect of the source's statement rather
than of its transcription.

The last line of the source's proof observes further that `S/(f₁, …, f_i)` is Cohen–Macaulay and
equidimensional of dimension `d - i`. That is a remark inside the proof and not part of the
assertion of the lemma, so it is not stated here.

Faithfulness to [StacksProject 00R9]:

1. No dead hypothesis. `hS` is Definition 00NC for `S` and `hequi` with `hd` is
   equidimensionality of dimension `d`; both are what force `dim(S_𝔪) = d` at every maximal
   ideal, the input to Proposition 00N6. `[Field k]`, `[Algebra k S]` and
   `[Algebra.FiniteType k S]` are the source's, and are load-bearing: they are what make
   `Spec S` Jacobson, hence what lets the closed points of `V(f₁, …, f_i)` control all of it, and
   they are also what makes `S` Noetherian. `hdim` is the inequality the conclusion upgrades to
   an equality, and `hf` excludes the empty locus (see above). `f` and `d` occur in the
   conclusion.
2. The scalars and base are the source's. `S` is an algebra over the field `k` and of finite type
   over `k`; the elements `f₁, …, f_i` are elements of `S` itself, the ideal they generate is an
   ideal of `S`, and the localizations are localizations of `S` at its own primes. All depths and
   dimensions in `hS` are taken over the local ring `S_𝔭` itself, not over `S` or over `k`.
3. The rings and locus the source names are pinned. `V(f₁, …, f_i)` is pinned as
   `Ideal.ofList f` through `ringKrullDim_quotient`, and `𝔮 ∈ V(f₁, …, f_i)` as
   `Ideal.ofList f ≤ q`; `S_𝔮` is pinned by `IsLocalization.AtPrime Sq q` through the canonical
   `algebraMap S Sq`, and the sequence whose regularity is asserted is the image
   `f.map (algebraMap S Sq)` of the source's own elements under that map, not some sequence
   assumed to exist.
4. The instantiation is not degenerate. Take `k` any field, `S = k[x, y]` (Cohen–Macaulay, and
   `Spec S` irreducible of dimension `d = 2`), `f = [x]`: then `S ⧸ (x) ≅ k[y]` has dimension
   `1`, so `hdim` reads `1 + 1 ≤ 2`, and the conclusion is that `x` is a nonzerodivisor in
   `k[x, y]_𝔮` for every prime `𝔮 ∋ x`, including the height one prime `(x)` itself and the
   maximal ideals `(x, g(y))`. Both conclusions have content: for `f = [x, x]` the hypothesis
   `hdim` fails (`dim V(x, x) + 2 = 1 + 2 > 2`), matching the fact that `x, x` is not a regular
   sequence, so the statement is not vacuously true.

